Erythema Multiforme
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Target/iris lesions with three concentric zones, Acral extensor distribution, Preceding HSV/medication trigger

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-018
Reason for Visit: Sudden eruption of widespread, burning, target-like skin lesions causing severe anxiety regarding an acute allergic reaction or systemic illness.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented an acute, rapidly progressive skin eruption of "bullseye" lesions spreading from the hands to the trunk, accompanied by mild malaise. Urgent assessment was required to differentiate this from a life-threatening severe cutaneous adverse reaction (e.g., Stevens-Johnson Syndrome/Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis), evaluate mucosal involvement, and provide diagnostic clarity before the next available in-hours appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My hands and arms broke out in these weird bullseye spots this morning, and now they are spreading to my chest. They feel hot and burning."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a sudden onset of a skin eruption that began 24 hours ago and has peaked in intensity. The rash initially appeared on the extensor surfaces of the acral extremities (hands and wrists) and is demonstrating centripetal spread to the trunk. The lesions are highly characteristic, described as target or iris lesions consisting of three distinct concentric colour zones. The patient complains of mild localized burning and mild pruritus. They deny severe systemic symptoms, high fever, or significant pain. Upon questioning, the patient notes a preceding history of a "cold sore" (Herpes Simplex Virus infection) on the lip approximately 10 days ago. They deny any new medications.
Functional Status: Independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme anxiety regarding the appearance and rapid spread of the rash.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 88 | Temp: 37.4 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 76 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, but visibly anxious regarding the cutaneous eruption.
Physical Exam: There is a symmetrical, widespread cutaneous eruption primarily involving the extensor surfaces of the hands, forearms, and extending to the anterior chest. The primary morphology consists of classic target/iris lesions. These feature a dark dusky central crust/blister, surrounded by a pale oedematous ring, and bordered by an outer erythematous halo. Careful examination of the oral mucosa, conjunctivae, and genitalia reveals absolutely no mucosal erosions or blistering (ruling out major variants). A healing herpetic crust is visible on the vermillion border of the lower lip. No sloughing of skin or Nikolsky sign is present.
Environmental Observations: Normal home environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current medications. No recent antibiotic or NSAID use.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Erythema Multiforme (Minor) secondary to recent HSV infection - Status: Acute
Differentials:

Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The complete absence of severe mucosal involvement (oral, ocular, genital), lack of high fever, and absence of extensive epidermal detachment or atypical target lesions with only two zones.

Urticaria (Hives) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The fixed, non-migratory nature of the lesions and the presence of three distinct concentric color zones with central epidermal damage, unlike the transient, evanescent wheals of urticaria.

Fixed Drug Eruption - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The generalized, symmetrical acral distribution and the clear preceding HSV trigger, contrasting with the solitary, localized plaques typically seen in fixed drug eruptions upon re-exposure.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with a classic, sudden-onset eruption of target lesions following a recent herpes simplex infection, hallmark for Erythema Multiforme Minor. Telephone triage appropriately flagged the rapid, generalized spread of the rash as requiring urgent evaluation to rule out early SJS/TEN or severe anaphylaxis. Immediate after-hours clinical assessment was vital to perform a thorough mucosal examination, confirm the minor variant of the disease, and, crucially, to provide immense clinical reassurance to a highly distressed patient. By confirming a benign, self-limiting etiology and ruling out a life-threatening drug reaction, an unnecessary emergency department presentation was successfully averted.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Provided extensive clinical reassurance regarding the self-limiting nature of the condition. Prescribed a mild topical corticosteroid (Hydrocortisone 1% cream) BD for any localized itchy/burning lesions. Advised simple analgesia if required.
Positive Behavioral Change: By applying cool, damp washcloths to the burning areas rather than scratching them, you will calm the nerve endings and prevent accidental damage to the skin surface. By focusing on the fact that this is your body's safe, albeit dramatic, immune response to a common cold sore, you can let go of the anxiety and allow the rash to fade naturally over the next two weeks.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7-10 days to monitor resolution, or earlier if symptoms change.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If you develop any painful blisters inside your mouth, on your lips, in your eyes, or in your genital area, or if you develop a high fever and the skin begins to peel off in large sheets, present immediately to the Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: While this is a minor reaction, the lesions can occasionally take weeks to completely fade.
Clinical Course: The rash has likely peaked and will gradually darken and fade over the next 1-3 weeks. It may leave temporary dark marks that will eventually resolve.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Monitor your mouth and eyes carefully. Any new pain, ulcers, or blistering in these areas requires an immediate emergency medical review.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: This dramatic rash is actually an allergic-type reaction to the cold sore virus you had 10 days ago. Your immune system is reacting to fragments of the virus, but it is not a live infection in the skin, and it is not dangerous.
Actionable Steps: Do not pop any small blisters that form in the center of the targets. Use the mild steroid cream strictly for comfort.

SMS INFORMATION

Assessed for sudden target-like rash. Diagnosed Erythema Multiforme (Minor) triggered by recent cold sore. No mucosal involvement. Routine GP review in 7 days.

SMS EDUCATION

Rash is a safe, self-limiting immune reaction. Apply steroid cream thinly for comfort. Seek urgent ED care if painful sores develop in mouth, eyes, or genitals.
